--
-- Test Description
-- The purpose of this test is to ensure the transmit functionality of the 
-- GEM works properly with the dma.
-- 
-- Functional Coverage
-- � Ensures the dma block can transmit frames of various lengths starting
--   at differing addresses.
-- � Ensures that the dma block reads words at a time and cannot roll over 
--   a 16-byte boundary. 
-- � Ensures that the dma block behaves properly for all possible 
--   combinations of start and stop addresses for the transmit buffers. 
-- � Ensures that the various transmit control and status registers are 
--   working properly.
-- � Ensures the back pressure functionality of the GEM works properly.
-- 
-- 
-- Detailed Test Description
-- 
--  1. Initialise suitable wait states for the test.
--  2. enable transmit to force transition in INTERFRAME_GAP_INIT for 
--     code coverage
--  3. Initialise GEM for 10 or 100-mode operation and for transmitting frames.
--  4. Check status registers.
--  5. Queue up a 48-byte frame with bit 16 set in word one of the transmit
--     buffer. Therefore, the GEM should just transmit the 48-byte frame without
--     appending any padding and crc.
--  6. Queue up a 2047 byte (85 byte for 10 mode) frame with bit 16 set in 
--     word one of the transmit buffer. Therefore, the GEM should just transmit
--     the 2047-byte frame without appending any padding and crc.
--  7. Transmit a frame where the descriptor indicates a buffer start address of
--     98765005. This forces the TX DMA to use an offset of 1. Therefore
--     Buffer data reads should begin at 98765004 (32 bit aligned), but the 
--     lowest byte should be ignored and not be present in the transmitted data.
--  8. Transmit a frame where the descriptor indicates a buffer start address of
--     9876500a. This forces the TX DMA to use an offset of 2. Therefore
--     Buffer data reads should begin at 98765008 (32 bit aligned), but the 2
--     lowest bytes should be ignored and not be present in the transmitted
--     data.
--  9. Transmit a frame where the descriptor indicates a buffer start address of
--     9876500f. This forces the TX DMA to use an offset of 3. Therefore
--     Buffer data reads should begin at 9876500c (32 bit aligned), but the 3
--     lowest bytes should be ignored and not be present in the transmitted
--     data.
-- 10. Queue up a 8-byte frame not including padding and crc (Length field 
--     in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 8 byte frame).  Therefore,
--     the GEM should transmit the 8-byte frame and append padding and crc
--     to make the frame minimum frame size.
-- 11. Transmit a frame where the descriptor indicates a buffer start address of
--     98765009. This forces the TX DMA to use an offset of 1. Therefore
--     Buffer data reads should begin at 98765008 (32 bit aligned), but the
--     lowest byte should be ignored and not be present in the transmitted
--     data.
--     This time the frame data is only 6 bytes long, and hence only two
--     DMA data reads are required. The frame will be padded to min frame size.
-- 12. Transmit a frame where the descriptor indicates a buffer start address of
--     9876500e. This forces the TX DMA to use an offset of 2. Therefore
--     Buffer data reads should begin at 9876500c (32 bit aligned), but the 2
--     lowest bytes should be ignored and not be present in the transmitted
--     data.
--     This time the frame data is only 3 bytes long, and but two DMA data 
--     reads are still required. The frame will be padded to min frame size.
-- 13. Check TX statistics at this point.
-- 14. Queue up a 48-byte frame not including padding and crc (Length field
--     in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 48 byte frame).
--     Therefore, the GEM should transmit the 48-byte frame and append
--     padding and crc to make the frame minimum frame size.
-- 15. Queue up a 64-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 64-byte frame.
-- 16. Queue up a 65-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 65-byte frame.
-- 17. Queue up a 66-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 66-byte frame.
-- 18. Queue up a 67-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 67-byte frame.
-- 19. Queue up a 68-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 68-byte frame.
-- 20. Queue up a 69-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 69-byte frame.
-- 21. Queue up a 70-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 70-byte frame.
-- 22. Queue up a 71-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 71-byte frame.
-- 23. Queue up a 72-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 72-byte frame.
-- 24. Queue up a 73-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 73-byte frame.
-- 25. Queue up a 74-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 74-byte frame.
-- 26. Queue up a 75-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 75-byte frame.
-- 27. Queue up a 76-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 76-byte frame.
-- 28. Queue up a 77-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 77-byte frame.
-- 29. Queue up a 78-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 78-byte frame.
-- 30. Queue up a 79-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 79-byte frame.
-- 31. Queue up a 80-byte frame including crc. The GEM should transmit
--     the 80-byte frame.
-- 32. Queue up a 49-byte frame not including padding and crc (Length
--     field in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 49 byte frame).
--     Force the GEM to append padding and crc to make the frame minimum 
--     frame size.
-- 33. Queue up a 50-byte frame not including padding and crc (Length
--     field in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 50 byte frame).
--     Force the GEM to append padding and crc to make the frame minimum 
--     frame size.
-- 34. Queue up a 51-byte frame not including padding and crc (Length
--     field in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 51 byte frame).
--     Force the GEM to append padding and crc to make the frame minimum 
--     frame size.
-- 35. Check status and statistics registers and ensure that the above
--     operations were handled properly.
-- 36. Disable transmit.
-- 37. Try writing tx_go whilst TX is still disabled, and verify that nothing 
--     happens.
-- 38. Enable RX and try writing tx_go again. Again verify that nothing happens.
-- 39. Enable transmit.
-- 40. Queue up a 1-byte frame not including padding and crc (Length field
--     in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 1 byte frame).
--     Therefore, the GEM should transmit the 1-byte frame and append
--     padding and crc to make the frame minimum frame size.
-- 41. Queue up a 2-byte frame not including padding and crc (Length field
--     in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 2 byte frame).
--     Therefore, the GEM should transmit the 2-byte frame and append
--     padding and crc to make the frame minimum frame size.
-- 42. Queue up a 3-byte frame not including padding and crc (Length field
--     in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 3 byte frame).
--     Therefore, the GEM should transmit the 3-byte frame and append
--     padding and crc to make the frame minimum frame size.
-- 43. Queue up a 4-byte frame not including padding and crc (Length field
--     in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 4 byte frame).
--     Therefore, the GEM should transmit the 4-byte frame and append
--     padding and crc to make the frame minimum frame size.
-- 44. Queue up a broadcast frame of 6 bytes not including pad and crc (Length
--     field in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 6 byte frame).
--     Therefore, the GEM should transmit the 6-byte frame and append
--     padding and crc to make the frame minimum frame size. This should
--     be recognised as a broadcast frame in the statistics.
-- 45. Queue up a broadcast frame of 6 bytes not including pad and crc (Length
--     field in the buffer descriptor should indicate a 6 byte frame).
--     Therefore, the GEM should transmit the 6-byte frame and append
--     padding and crc to make the frame minimum frame size. This time the
--     last bit of the address is corrupted (0xfffffffffff7) so that this 
--     should be recognised as a multicast frame in the statistics.
-- 46. Check status and statistics registers and ensure the above operations
--     were handled properly.
-- 
